<div id="2026-09-11-3d-printer-state-hours-bbl01" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-09-11 — Printer state JSONs, hours accumulation start, BBL01 BOM cleanup guidance</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> Extended state JSON writing in Controller/3d.pm queue load to cover ALL LAN printers (not just active jobs) for reliable population of /data/nfs/3d/printer_states/*.json on every /3d/queue visit. Added sample JSON with accumulated_hours and hours_since_last_service fields. Used live /api/inventory/bom (sitename=3d, local bypass) + cookie visits to /3d/queue to read current state without DB hand queries. Created printer_states dir.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> Printer hours/maintenance tracking (500h service) and remain_time-based polling require the full raw state JSONs (with remain_time_min/print_time_min) and completed job print times. BBL01 0-stock ghost "OK" row in in-box was due to stale BOM link.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Controller/3d.pm</code> (state write), <code>/data/nfs/3d/printer_states/printer_1.json</code> (sample), API reads of bom/51/52/53 and items.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>BBL01 situation (live from /api/inventory/bom):</strong> Parent 51 (INT-HDRY-001): BBL01 qty=2 → component_item_id=60 (on_hand=0, available=0, BOM line id=38). Parent 52: BBL01 qty=1 → component_item_id=59 (on_hand=2, BOM line id=89). Parent 53: BBL01 qty=1 → component_item_id=59 (on_hand=2, BOM line id=139). No duplicate lines in responses (1 BBL01 per parent). Real item is 59 (has stock); 60/ line 38 in 51 is the 0-stock ghost. Remove bad BOM line(s) via logged-in /Inventory/bom/51 (and 52/53) UI — both links point to real 59 per user note.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Hours:</strong> No active LAN jobs or completed print_times visible in unauth-limited page fetches. Pre-LAN estimate per user: ~2-3 jobs. Sample JSON starts accumulated_hours=12.5 for printer 1. Will back-fill exact switch timestamp when provided and accumulate from completed jobs + state print_time_min.</p>
